This keeps the first two positions fixed and searches for the third value with binary search. Sorting first lets the search use `-nums[i] - nums[j]` as the target. A set of vectors is used only to remove duplicate triples.

It is a little slower than the usual two-pointer scan, but it shows another way to use the sorted order. Sorting changes the input array.

For an input such as `[-2, 0, 2, 3]`, after fixing `-2` and `0`, the target is `2`, and binary search finds it at the last position.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> threeSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        set<vector<int>> uniqueTriples;
        int n = nums.size();

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 2; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n - 1; j++) {
                long long target = -static_cast<long long>(nums[i]) - nums[j];
                auto it = lower_bound(nums.begin() + j + 1, nums.end(), target);

                if (it != nums.end() && *it == target) {
                    uniqueTriples.insert({nums[i], nums[j], static_cast<int>(target)});
                }
            }
        }

        return vector<vector<int>>(uniqueTriples.begin(), uniqueTriples.end());
    }
};
```

When the array is empty or has fewer than three elements, the loop bounds prevent any search. The initial sort costs `O(n log n)`. There are `O(n^2)` pairs, and each binary search costs `O(log n)`, giving `O(n^2 log n)` time. The extra space is `O(1)` besides the output and duplicate set.

Sorting makes the duplicate handling much easier. After fixing `nums[i]`, one pointer starts just after it and another starts at the end. If the current sum is too small, move the left pointer. If it is too large, move the right pointer.

For example, with `[-1, 0, 1, 2, -1, -4]`, sorting gives `[-4, -1, -1, 0, 1, 2]`. At `i = 1`, the values `-1`, `0`, and `1` make a valid triple. The repeated `-1` at the next outer position is skipped.

This version sorts the input list in place, so the original order is changed.

```python
class Solution:
    def threeSum(self, nums: list) -> list[list]:
        nums.sort()
        result = []
        n = len(nums)

        for i in range(n - 2):
            if nums[i] > 0:
                break
            if i > 0 and nums[i] == nums[i - 1]:
                continue

            left = i + 1
            right = n - 1

            while left < right:
                total = nums[i] + nums[left] + nums[right]

                if total == 0:
                    result.append([nums[i], nums[left], nums[right]])
                    left += 1
                    right -= 1

                    while left < right and nums[left] == nums[left - 1]:
                        left += 1
                    while left < right and nums[right] == nums[right + 1]:
                        right -= 1
                elif total < 0:
                    left += 1
                else:
                    right -= 1

        return result
```

An empty list and a list with only one or two values return an empty result naturally. Sorting costs `O(n log n)`, and the scan for each fixed value costs `O(n)`, so the total time is `O(n^2)`. The extra working space is `O(1)` apart from the answer.

Here the outer loop chooses the first number. For the remaining part of the array, a `HashSet` stores values already seen. If `-nums[i] - nums[j]` is in that set, the three values form a zero-sum triple.

The triple is sorted before being added to a result set. That keeps the output free of duplicates even when the input has repeated values. The input array stays unchanged.

A small dry run: for `[-1, 0, 1]`, fixing `-1` and seeing `0` makes the needed value `1`. Since `1` is already in the set, `[-1, 0, 1]` is saved.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> threeSum(int[] nums) {
        Set<List<Integer>> uniqueTriples = new HashSet<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 2; i++) {
            Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();

            for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {
                int needed = -nums[i] - nums[j];

                if (seen.contains(needed)) {
                    int[] triple = {nums[i], nums[j], needed};
                    Arrays.sort(triple);
                    uniqueTriples.add(Arrays.asList(
                        triple[0], triple[1], triple[2]
                    ));
                }

                seen.add(nums[j]);
            }
        }

        return new ArrayList<>(uniqueTriples);
    }
}
```

The result for no possible triple is an empty list. The two loops and average constant-time set checks give `O(n^2)` time. The set used for each outer iteration holds `O(n)` values, while the stored answer can also take `O(n^2)` space in the worst case.

A direct version is useful for checking the idea first. Try every choice of three different positions and keep the triple when its sum is zero. A `set` removes duplicate answers after sorting each triple.

This is slow for large arrays, but the code is easy to compare with faster versions. It does not change `nums`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> threeSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        set<vector<int>> uniqueTriples;
        int n = nums.size();

        for (int first = 0; first < n; first++) {
            for (int second = first + 1; second < n; second++) {
                for (int third = second + 1; third < n; third++) {
                    long long sum = static_cast<long long>(nums[first])
                                  + nums[second] + nums[third];

                    if (sum == 0) {
                        vector<int> triple = {
                            nums[first], nums[second], nums[third]
                        };
                        sort(triple.begin(), triple.end());
                        uniqueTriples.insert(triple);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return vector<vector<int>>(uniqueTriples.begin(), uniqueTriples.end());
    }
};
```

For an empty array or an array with fewer than three values, all loops finish without adding anything. The three nested loops take `O(n^3)` time. The set can store `O(n^2)` different triples in the worst case, not counting the returned result.

## Intuition
Idea simple hai: array ko sort kar do, taaki numbers ek order mein aa jaayein. Ab jab hum ek number fix karte hain, toh baaki do numbers ko dhoondhna easy ho jaata hai using two pointers. Sorted hone ki wajah se humein pata hota hai ki sum chhota hai ya bada, isliye hum logically pointers ko move kar sakte hain instead of random trials. Saath hi, same values ko skip karke hum duplicate triplets se bach jaate hain — matlab clean aur efficient solution.

## Approach
Sabse pehle array ko sort karo. Phir ek loop se index i ko fix karo (0 se n-2 tak), aur duplicate i values ko skip karo. Agar kabhi nums[i] > 0 ho jaaye, toh wahi break kar do, kyunki aage jaakar sum zero hona possible hi nahi hai. Ab i fix hone ke baad, two pointers lagao: j = i+1 aur k = n-1. Jab tak j < k, teen numbers ka sum check karo — agar sum chhota hai toh j aage badhao, agar bada hai toh k peeche lao. Jab sum zero mile, triplet store karo aur j aur k dono ko move karke duplicates skip kar do. Is tarah saare unique triplets efficiently mil jaate hain.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
   public:
    vector<vector<int>> threeSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<vector<int>> ans; //2D array
        int n = nums.size();

        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        // i ko n tak jane ki jarurat nahi hai
        // kyunki (n-1) pe toh k hai
        // aur j hamesha i se ek kadam aage hota hai!
        // is liye i(0 -> n-2)

        // agar tum i(0 -> n) loge na
        // toh j=i+1(out of bound hojaega!)
        // aur last pe toh hamesha k hai na

        for(int i=0; i<n-2; i++){
            if(i>0 && nums[i] == nums[i-1]) // skip duplicates
                continue;

            if(nums[i] > 0) break; // ans exist he nahi karta!    

            int j = i + 1; // st
            int k = n - 1; // end

            // two pointers
            while(j<k){
                int sum = nums[i] + nums[j] + nums[k];
                if(sum<0) j++;
                else if(sum>0) k--;
                else{
                    // found a triplet
                    vector<int>temp = {nums[i],nums[j],nums[k]};
                    ans.push_back(temp);

                    j++;
                    k--;

                    //skip duplicates
                    while(j<k && nums[j] == nums[j-1]) j++;
                    while(j<k && nums[k] == nums[k-1]) k--;
                }
            }
        }

    return ans;
    }
};
```

## Complexity
Array ko sort karne mein O(n log n) time lagta hai. Uske baad hum ek loop i ke liye chalate hain aur har i ke liye two pointers (j aur k) use karte hain, jo milkar poore array ko ek hi baar traverse karte hain. Isliye overall time complexity O(n²) ho jaati hai (n²  >>  n log n). Simple words mein bolein toh brute force O(n³) se kaafi better hai, kyunki hum sorted array aur two pointers ka smart use kar rahe hain.

**Space Complexity:**
Extra space ke naam par hum sirf kuch variables (i, j, k) aur result array use karte hain. Sorting in-place hoti hai, isliye input ke alawa koi additional data structure nahi lagta. Is wajah se auxiliary space complexity O(1) hoti hai, aur jo space answer store karne mein lagta hai woh output ka part hota hai, use usually count nahi kiya jaata.

